<div class="episode"><b>03</b> NBA TIME MACHINE <span>•</span> 1986–87 × 2025–26</div>
<h1>We Tried to Find the Next <em>Charles Barkley.</em> The Model Kept Picking Centers.</h1>
<p class="dek">He was too short to play his position. He dominated it anyway. We asked the numbers who looks like him today, and the answer wasn't who we expected.</p>
<div class="byline">BY <strong>DIVINE OSAS-EGBE</strong> &nbsp;·&nbsp; OCTOBER 30, 2026 &nbsp;·&nbsp; ANTICIPATE ORIGINAL RESEARCH</div>
</div>
<div>
<figure class="hero-visual" style="margin:0">
<img src={imageUrl} alt="Charles Barkley wearing his Philadelphia 76ers jersey in a mid-1980s game, holding the ball high" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" />
<div class="img-note"><strong>34.</strong><span>THE OUTLIER · PHILADELPHIA</span></div>
</figure>
<p class="img-credit">Archival photograph: Andrew D. Bernstein / NBAE, via <a href="https://photostore.nba.com/featured/1-charles-barkley-andrew-d-bernstein.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NBA Photo Store</a>. Image is an editorial reference and remains the photographer's copyright.</p>
</div>
</section>
<div class="numbers" aria-label="Barkley's 1986–87 production">
<div class="number"><strong>23.0</strong><small>POINTS PER GAME</small></div><div class="number"><strong>14.6</strong><small>REBOUNDS PER GAME · NBA LEADER</small></div><div class="number"><strong>66.0%</strong><small>TRUE SHOOTING · NBA LEADER</small></div><div class="number"><strong>6'6"</strong><small>LISTED HEIGHT · OFTEN REPORTED SHORTER</small></div>
</div>
<section class="story">
<article class="copy">
<span class="section-index">01 / A DIFFERENT KIND OF BIG MAN</span>
<p class="opening">A player listed at 6-foot-6, and widely believed to be even shorter, battling seven-footers in one of basketball's most physical, big-man-oriented eras. That's Charles Barkley.</p>
<p>He didn't need a collection of flashy moves. He wasn't interested in settling for a jump shot. He would turn his back, lower his shoulder, and power through players half a foot taller than him. Then he'd do it again. And again. Until somebody found a way to stop him.</p>
<p>He became so closely associated with that kind of back-down basketball that when the NBA introduced a five-second back-to-the-basket rule in 1999, Barkley was one of the players named in contemporary reporting, alongside Mark Jackson. The rule wasn't created for Barkley alone. But think about what it means to have your playing style mentioned in the explanation for changing how an entire league plays.</p>
<p><strong>How do you explain that to someone who never watched him?</strong> How do you describe an undersized power forward bullying some of the greatest big men basketball has ever seen?</p>
<p>Statistically, players like Barkley exist. He averaged 23 points and almost 15 rebounds in 1986–87. Incredible numbers, but other great big men have put up incredible numbers too. Physically, undersized power forwards exist. We've seen Draymond Green and P.J. Tucker prove that a frontcourt player doesn't need to be seven feet tall.</p>
<p>But a player who combines that physical profile <em>with</em> Barkley's production? That's what makes him special. And that's what makes this investigation interesting.</p>
<h2><span class="section-index">02 / THE SEASON</span>The numbers are ridiculous. The context makes them worse.</h2>
<p>In 1986–87, Barkley was 23 years old. He averaged <strong>23.0 points, 14.6 rebounds, and 4.9 assists</strong> in 40.3 minutes per game for Philadelphia. He led the entire league with 5.7 offensive rebounds per game. That is 390 offensive rebounds in 68 games, almost six extra chances every night before the defense could finish the possession.</p>
<p>And for a player remembered for strength and physicality, maybe the wildest statistic is his efficiency: <strong>66.0% true shooting</strong>, compared with the league's 53.8%. That's a <strong>+12.2 percentage-point</strong> advantage over his own era. He led the league in true shooting percentage, rebounds per game and offensive rebounds per game, while being one of its primary offensive options.</p>
<p>His rebounding wasn't just the product of playing 40 minutes. He collected 20.8% of available rebounds while on the floor. His 16.7% offensive rebounding rate meant he was getting his hands on possessions at the end where it is hardest to win the ball. Size alone doesn't explain it. Timing, leverage and a willingness to wrestle for space do. </p><p>There's a difference between being an efficient finisher who only gets the ball near the rim and being a player who scores, creates, rebounds and dictates the physical terms of a game. Barkley was doing all of that. Our model can see the production. The harder question is whether it can see the player.</p>
<div class="pullquote">The box score can find you another 23-and-15 player. Finding someone who got there the way Barkley did is a different problem.</div>
<h2><span class="section-index">03 / WHY THE GLASS MATTERS</span>Rebounding is the whole point.</h2>
<p>When we talk about Barkley being undersized, we don't just mean he survived at power forward. He <strong>led the NBA in rebounds per game</strong>, beating centers who had half a foot on him. He also led the league in <strong>offensive rebounds per game</strong>. That's not a side note to his legend. It's the most direct proof that strength, timing and positioning translated into extra possessions for his team.</p>
<p>Rebound percentage offers another way to see it. Barkley grabbed <strong>20.8% of all available rebounds</strong> while he played. That is extraordinary for someone his height, and it is the number we need to hold his modern comparisons against. A comparable build is one thing. Actually controlling the glass is another.</p>
<h2><span class="section-index">04 / THE RESULTS</span>We gave the model 279 candidates. Four of its top five were centers.</h2>
<p>We compared Barkley's 1986–87 season with every player who cleared 1,000 minutes in our 2025–26 dataset. We adjusted shooting efficiency relative to each player's league and compared scoring, assists, rebounds, usage, steals, blocks, size and position. The closest overall match was <strong>Jarrett Allen</strong>. Jalen Duren finished second. Then Day'Ron Sharpe, Marvin Bagley III and Sandro Mamukelashvili.</p>
<p>The model sees Barkley's elite rebounding and efficient interior scoring, so it gravitates toward big men. Jalen Duren's 20.9% total rebound rate nearly mirrors Barkley's 20.8%. Jarrett Allen finishes first overall because he brings efficient finishing, strong rebounding and a closer combined physical profile. But neither plays the same role on offense that Barkley did.</p>
<p>One name was missing for a completely different reason: <strong>Domantas Sabonis</strong>. He played only 564 minutes in 2025–26, under our 1,000-minute cutoff. But his complete <strong>2024–25 season</strong> deserves a separate test against Barkley.</p>
</article>
