<section class="prosewide">
<span class="section-index">04 / ZION'S CASE</span>
<h2>Maybe the most Barkley-like player isn't the closest match.</h2>
<p>Before running the model, Zion Williamson feels like a reasonable answer. An undersized power forward who can make contact look like a mismatch in his favour. A player who doesn't need a towering frame to get defenders moving backward. He looks the part of a modern Barkley comparison in a way Jarrett Allen simply doesn't.</p>
<p>But the model puts Zion <strong>11th</strong>, not first. He scored 33.8 points per 100 possessions in 2025–26, compared with Barkley's 28.2 in 1986–87. Yet Barkley grabbed 20.8% of available rebounds while Zion grabbed 10.3%. Barkley's relative true shooting was +12.2 points; Zion's was +6.3. For all the visual similarities, the numbers don't line up neatly.</p>
<p>And what about Allen? He scores 27.2 points per 100 possessions, his efficiency is a strong +8.8 points relative to his league, and he grabs 17.2% of available rebounds. That is closer to Barkley's statistical production. But Allen is a center who plays a very different offensive role. Barkley could collect a defensive rebound and start a possession himself. His 19.0% assist rate was almost twice Allen's 9.7%.</p>
<p><strong>Jalen Duren is the other fascinating case.</strong> His 20.9% total rebound rate is nearly identical to Barkley's 20.8%. He is even closer than Allen on the statistical component of our model. But he is listed at 6-foot-10, and the full physical component gives Allen the overall edge.</p>
<div class="split-cards">
<div class="split-card"><h3>Jarrett Allen</h3><p><strong>Closest overall model match.</strong> Efficient interior production and strong rebounding. Less offensive creation than Barkley, and a different physical role.</p></div>
<div class="split-card"><h3>Zion Williamson</h3><p><strong>The stylistic wildcard.</strong> A shorter, powerful forward with a natural interior game. Far less rebounding, with a different scoring-versus-creation balance.</p></div>
</div>
<p>Neither answer is embarrassing. They're answers to different questions. <strong>Allen is closer to Barkley's measurable profile. Zion might feel closer to his playing style.</strong> Confusing those statements is how people turn a similarity score into a basketball argument it was never built to win.</p>
<span class="section-index">05 / THE VERDICT</span>
<h2>There isn't a new Barkley. There's a good reason we keep looking.</h2>
<p>At first glance, seeing a list of centers might make you think the model missed the entire point of Barkley. But maybe it captured half of the point perfectly.</p>
<p>Charles Barkley produced like a center without having the body of one. The model looks at the numbers and reaches for players who finish efficiently and dominate the glass. Watch the film, and you see a shorter power forward using leverage, quickness, touch and sheer force to manufacture the same results. The numbers reflect the outcome. They don't preserve the path he took to get there.</p>
<p>That doesn't make the model useless. It tells us what the model is actually good at. It can show us statistical relatives, expose gaps in production, and force us to ask why a comparison feels wrong. The eye test can push back against a spreadsheet. The spreadsheet can challenge what we thought we saw.</p>
<p><strong>And Barkley remains difficult to reproduce because his greatness lived in both.</strong> The dominant big-man output, and the body that wasn't supposed to produce it. That combination is why, almost forty years later, we're still searching for somebody who looks like him.</p>
</section>

<section class="method" id="methodology">
<h2>How we built this</h2>
<p>We compared Charles Barkley's <strong>1986–87 regular season</strong> against 279 players who logged at least 1,000 minutes in <strong>2025–26</strong>. The statistical component uses points per 100 possessions, era-relative true shooting, assist percentage, total rebound percentage, usage percentage, steal percentage and block percentage. Each numerical difference is divided by the standard deviation of the 2025–26 eligible group. A second component compares listed height and weight; the final one penalizes a different broad position category (guard, forward or center).</p>
<p>The published formula weights those components 70%, 20% and 10%. The interactive preset tool recalculates the complete 279-player ranking when those weights change. We do not score three-point percentage, because three-point shot opportunity differs radically between Barkley's early career and 2025–26. The formula measures profile resemblance, not individual greatness, film-based technique, creation type, defensive impact or historical achievement.</p>
<p><strong>Data notes:</strong> Barkley's 1986–87 per-game figures, efficiency and advanced rates were checked against Basketball-Reference. The model uses his listed 198 cm / 252 lb measurements; published accounts report he may actually have been shorter. The 2025–26 dataset is a frozen cleaned research snapshot with player statistics attributed to Basketball-Reference and bio measurements derived from NBA Stats exports. Source revisions, measurement uncertainty, one-season comparisons and the arbitrary weight choices can change the rankings. The ranking is exploratory, not independently validated as a scouting metric.</p>
